This paper sits between the high-quality T2I benchmarks (6-7.33) and the weak novelty-inflation papers (3-4.25). The task formulation, dataset construction, and empirical findings are legitimate contributions that merit a score above the rejected novelty-inflation papers. However, the two major weaknesses (invalid probabilistic framing, overclaimed abstract) pull it below the accepted T2I benchmarks. The direct predecessor (Baryshnikov & Ryabinin) scored 6.0 but was rejected; this paper extends it with more metrics and models but inherits similar weaknesses. The overclaiming in the abstract and theoretical framing is more severe here than in typical 6.0 papers. I place this at 5.0 — it has real empirical contributions that should be presented more honestly, but the methodological overclaiming prevents a clear acceptance signal.

