## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract & Introduction:** The title claims "Serial Attention Routing" but nothing in the method is serial—the experts are combined via a softmax-weighted sum and passed to the next layer. If "serial" refers to the experts being placed before attention computation, this is the same as any standard LoRA application and is not "serial routing." The title mischaracterizes the mechanism.

**§2 Related Work:** Adequate coverage, though the framing of existing methods as producing "shallow output fusion" is rhetorically loaded rather than precisely argued.

**§3.1-3.2 (Method):** The method itself is straightforward: apply top-k MoE routing over LoRA experts on attention projection matrices. The mathematical formulation is standard. Equation 5 contains garbled text that appears partly parser-artifact and partly author error ("the of the null is a result to α, λ is a well-conditioned Gaussian Gaussian distribution"). The "information bottleneck" perspective on routing (§3.3) is invoked but the connection is loose—the entropy term is just standard entropy regularization on a categorical distribution, not derived from rate-distortion theory.

**§3.3 (RSL):** The gradient derivation in Eqs. 6-8 is the standard entropy gradient under a simplex constraint. The claim that this provides "strong convexity" and "well-conditioned optimization" is deferred to Appendix A.1 without a sketch. Equation 10 defines "token-aware" routing as having positive variance, which is vacuous for any non-degenerate router. The formal contribution of RSL over standard auxiliary-loss-plus-entropy-regularization is not clearly delineated.

**§4.1 (Setup):** Using DeepSeek-R1 to evaluate MedicalQA is unusual and potentially problematic. LLM-as-judge evaluation without calibration against human annotators introduces an uncontrolled variable.

**§4.2 (Table 2):** LoRAHub is a gradient-free, few-shot method. Comparing it against a method that uses full training data is not a fair comparison and inflates the apparent gains.

**§4.5 (Analysis):** The ablations cover rank (r), top-K, and expert load visualization, but miss the key ablation identified above (attention vs. FFN placement). Figure 4 shows per-task expert specialization, which is useful but anecdotal without a comparison to standard auxiliary loss.

**§5 (Conclusion):** Acknowledges limitations (fixed top-K, uniform application across layers) that should have been addressed experimentally rather than deferred to future work.

