## Strengths

- The plug-and-play regime (§3.2), where frozen LoRAs from external repositories are routed with only 2K data, is practically appealing and the Flan-T5 experiments (Table 3) show it works reasonably well.
- The RSL motivation—standard auxiliary loss drives routing toward uniformity, suppressing task-aware specialization—is intuitively correct and the variance analysis (Eq. 10, Figure 4) qualitatively illustrates the problem.
- The framework is genuinely architecture-agnostic, demonstrated by experiments on the Falcon-Mamba SSM model (Table 2), which most LoRA-MoE methods cannot handle.

