## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Outperforms both parameter-matched and computation-matched baselines consistently across scales**: Table 1 shows Thoughtbubbles (κ=4L) achieves 19.74 perplexity on OpenWebText at 772M vs. 20.90 for Copy-5 (a computation-matched baseline), and strikingly, the 319M model (20.23) outperforms the 772M baseline (21.22). Improvements hold across LAMBADA and HellaSwag on both datasets at all three scales, with the only exceptions being BLiMP and PIQA at some scales.

2. **Unsupervised computation allocation emerges at interpretable regions without any explicit supervision**: Figure 5 demonstrates that cumulative forking scores correlate with higher-entropy tokens, measured both by the forking model itself and an independently-trained baseline LM. This directly validates the paper's claim that adaptive computation is learned from LM loss alone, with no auxiliary regularization or supervision signal.

3. **Enables pretraining-time adaptive computation, unlike prior pause/thinking token methods**: While prior work (Pfau et al., 2024; Goyal et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2025) requires manually inserting explicit tokens before computation begins, Thoughtbubbles' forking layers (Section 2.3) learn where to fork and delete mid-network based on cumulative scores propagated through layers, allowing intermediate-layer computation allocation. This is the first method that makes parallel adaptive computation learnable from standard LM pretraining loss alone (Section 3.1).

### Supporting strengths

1. **Forked streams are meaningfully utilized by the parent token, not merely occupying capacity**: Figure 4 shows the rightmost ("original") token attends to its forked children with attention scores more than an order of magnitude higher than to other tokens, confirming that forks contribute substantively to computing the final output rather than being wasted compute.

2. **Careful attention to inference-time deployment**: The analysis of distribution shift between blockwise forward passes and autoregressive generation (Figure 6, Section 5.1), along with the proposed dynamic budget mitigation (κ proportional to input size), addresses a practical concern that many architecture papers overlook.

3. **Nuanced analysis of the entropy–computation relationship**: The concave parabolic relationship in Figure 5—where moderate-uncertainty tokens receive the most computation while highest-uncertainty tokens receive less—provides insight into what the model learns is useful to compute, going beyond a simple "more entropy = more computation" finding.

## Summary

The paper introduces Thoughtbubbles, a transformer variant that enables unsupervised adaptive parallel computation during pretraining by learning to fork and delete residual streams based on cumulative importance scores, trained with only standard LM loss. The most important evidence supporting this contribution is Table 1, where Thoughtbubbles consistently outperforms both parameter-matched baselines and computation-matched baselines (Copy-3, Copy-5) across three model scales and two datasets on perplexity, LAMBADA, and HellaSwag—with the 319M model even surpassing the 772M baseline on OpenWebText perplexity—while Figure 5 demonstrates that the unsupervised forking mechanism learns to allocate computation at tokens with higher posterior entropy without any explicit supervision.

